WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.900
It's great to welcome to the program today.
Eric Kaufman, who is professor of politics

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.230
at Birkbeck. Yep.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:12.679
University of London in the UK. Also author
of white shift, populism, immigration and

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:17.930
the future of white majorities. Uh, Eric,
great to have you on today. Great to be here,

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:23.269
David. So let's, I mean how, what's the best
way when someone doesn't know about the discussions

00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:27.449
that are taking place in political circles
and anthropological circles in many circles

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:35.170
about the future of race divisions or or sort
of the racial makeup of modern Western countries

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:39.960
including the United States. What is the kind
of top line change that we're seeing and what

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.000
we expect racial makeup to look like in 25
and 50 years?

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.480
Well, many, many Americans are, are obviously
familiar with the figure of roughly half of

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:58.260
Americans won't be non Hispanic whites around
2050 that's, that's been known for a while.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.609
Less well known is that something similar
will occur at around the same time in Canada

00:01:02.609 --> 00:01:08.610
and New Zealand. Uh, the changes in Canada,
which is where I'm originally from, um, are

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:14.000
actually quite dramatic. Some Statistics Canada
demographers showed that in 2006 the population

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.600
was about 80% white, 20% minority. And then
by 2106 a hundred years later, the proportions

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.330
are, are more or less reversed, kind of give
you, it now counted as one of the faster moving

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:29.030
countries. But it gives you a sense of the
changes we're, we're really only now embarking

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:33.930
on and that we're really gonna reverberate
around the West in Europe. It will be towards

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:39.119
the end of the century, at least in western
Europe that the majority minority point we'll

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:43.899
will arrive. But this is, these are just a
sense of a set of quite monumental shifts

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:46.950
that are going to change our politics in great
ways.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:53.799
So what has been the, uh, sort of political
reaction as people learn about this coming

00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:58.700
change across countries? I mean, I know that
in the United States there's definitely a

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:05.159
right wing element that sees this as evidence
that whites are or soon will be an oppressed

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:11.861
minority. Potentially. This leads to arguments
about what immigration policy should be. It

00:02:11.861 --> 00:02:16.690
stems and connect stems from and connects
to a lot of other sort of policy issues. But

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:24.220
is there some general way in which this shift
is being sort of interpreted by left and right

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.380
wing elements of the different countries you
mentioned?

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:33.640
Yeah, I think that there is a general pattern.
The, the response amongst conservatives is

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:39.640
one of identity threat I think, which is what's
fundamentally driving, um, right wing populism

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:45.280
I think across Europe and also in the US and
elsewhere. I think on the left there is an

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:51.310
element of the left that is sort of seizing
upon this as a sort of signal or harbingers

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:56.310
or that some kind of new societies is emerging
and it's sort of been bold emboldening a kind

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:01.671
of multicultural, what I would think is almost
a millenarian view of, of the new society

00:03:01.671 --> 00:03:05.920
that's going to emerge sort of diverse and
equal. Um, and I think you've got a clash

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.600
of these two visions that's taking place.
And so part of this is not just the rise of

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.510
right wing populism but also a value polarization
as these visions clash.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:20.150
Can you talk about that a little bit? When
you say value polarization, uh, I think most

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:24.400
people understand what values are and they
understand polarization, but what, what are

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.940
we talking about more specifically when values
become polarized?

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:34.920
Yeah. Essentially what we know from political
psychology is that there are sort of deep

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:41.980
rooted dispositions that people have. Um,
put bluntly, essentially some people prefer

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:47.790
diversity difference change, other people
prefer order continuity. The president to

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:53.040
be like the past, that that is sort of between
a third and a half heritable. If we look at

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.870
the literature on psychological authoritarianism
or status quo conservatism by people like

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:03.450
Karen Stout Stenner. Um, and what this means
therefore is that as we see these demographic

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:08.790
shifts are going to be processed very differently
by people who are psychologically more conservative

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.590
than those who are more open to change or
who score high on something like that. The

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:19.251
big five trait openness to experience. So
what we're seeing then is this divide, some

00:04:19.251 --> 00:04:24.600
have called it open close to some of called
East somewheres anywheres. Uh, that cultural

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:30.360
value divide is reordering politics. It's
splitting political parties that were initially

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:37.110
formed on the basis of left versus right,
which are economic orientations around lower

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:42.200
tax, more redistribution that that older economic
cleavage in politics is being overlaid by

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.420
the stronger kind of open, close type cleavage.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:50.510
Yeah. Um, uh, Jonathan Heit has some interesting
work along these lines, a as well who is,

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:55.380
he's probably the person who I've read most
recently on these ideas of values now do the

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:00.000
values carry. So let's back up a little bit.
There have been a lot of discussions that

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.950
I've had on the program about how at face
value it doesn't necessarily correlate that

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:13.540
a political contingent that is for low taxes
would necessarily also be against abortion,

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:19.560
for example, and worried about so-called oppression
against whites as a result, as well as the

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:24.140
result of demographic shifts. Like it doesn't
appear that there is anything obvious that

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:30.190
is sort of weaving those positions together
into a defined political constituency, but

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:35.010
is there actually something sort of underneath
the surface that does inform why those political

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:40.790
positions end up sort of as part of the same
voting block or or is there not?

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:48.170
Well, yeah, you can get ideological construction
whereby positions which probably don't necessarily

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:54.290
align on the sort of psychological levels
such as free markets and restricted immigration.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:59.570
It's not obvious that those two things go
together at all, but you will have an ideological

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:04.681
package that's created by a right wing political
party or a left wing political party. They

00:06:04.681 --> 00:06:09.320
will yolked together things that may not fit
either philosophically or psychologically.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:16.730
What I would say, however, is that the pressures
from the sort of psychological value was groups

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:22.639
can reconfigure parties so far. Right. Parties
in Europe which used to be more low tax have

00:06:22.639 --> 00:06:27.900
shifted more in the direction of a welfare
state. Welfare protectionism. Even in the

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:33.140
u s you saw that one of the big predictors
of Trump's support us as opposed to support

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:38.350
for other primary candidates was actually
a relatively, uh, more liberal attitude to

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:39.710
things like Medicare.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:45.440
Um, so that sort of economically, slightly
to the left at, but culturally very concerned

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:50.780
about immigration. That group fractured the
Republican Party to some extent. So I think

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:56.200
even though, yes parties do provide these
menus of options, which are in many ways,

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:59.950
um, don't fit together very well, you can
get these movements that fracture that and

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:05.520
create new orientations amongst the parties.
So yeah, it's partly about how the, uh, political

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:11.820
actors slot into an fit into these dispositions.
I mean, so you could have imagined it could

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:17.970
be war on terror that could be mobilizing
that, that sort of, um, order seeking disposition

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:24.360
amongst voters. But then when the war on terror,
the Cold War fades, another division might

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.510
be mobilized. It might be the board, or for
example, um, so there are different ways of

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:33.690
plugging into these dispositions. The dispositions
are somewhat nonspecific. One thing we know,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:38.680
however, it's certainly immigration attitudes
are heavily, uh, extremely heavily correlated

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:40.460
with these value dispositions.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:45.650
I mean, I've looked at British and US data
and um, views on, for example, the death penalty

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:51.590
views on whether things in let's say things
in America were better in the past, especially

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:56.010
American culture was better in the past. That
has a very, very strong link to immigration

00:07:56.010 --> 00:08:01.460
attitude. So I think it's these, some psychological
dispositions which underlie the immigration

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:06.980
attitudes, which underlie this sort of populist
politics and the polarization. The polarization

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:13.170
also arises when the kind of left liberal
side reacts to the populist right side and

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:17.711
thinks they are morally retrograde. Uh, and
there's, I think the whole debate there, but

00:08:17.711 --> 00:08:23.830
that reaction to the rise of the, this right,
that provides, in addition to people responding

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:29.040
differently to the stimulus of diversity,
you also have this value division over, is

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.919
it even legitimate to have a conversation
about restricting immigration? Um, so that

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:38.400
adds just another layer to this polarizing
dynamic. It's, it's most extreme in America,

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:43.700
but we saw in the European elections recently
that in Europe, both populist right and the

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:49.950
green and liberal parties, uh, scored better
than they ever have. So that's kind of suggesting

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:52.830
Europe is kind of moving in that polarizing
direction.

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:59.360
So Eric, when it comes to the possible grievances
that one could have, um, sort of under the

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:04.140
umbrella of right wing populism with this
racial shift that we are, are starting to

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:10.640
see with the idea of possibly losing status,
which we know was a great motivator for some

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:15.940
Trump voters in 2016 if you had to sort of
steal man as much as possible to make the

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:22.170
best possible case that some or one of those
grievances is sort of backed in historical

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:27.320
realities or empirical realities. Do, do any
of those grievances stand up?

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:33.020
I actually think they do, and this may be
a place we might disagree is this. I think

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:37.710
that there's a real difference in the survey
data. For example, in the psychological literature

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:44.610
between um, hatred about groups, kind of racism
and attachment to own group. Um, now our attachment

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:48.750
to own group can also lead to racism if it
leads you to discriminate. But I think there's

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:53.950
an important distinction there. So people
can be attached to being a member of an ethnic

00:09:53.950 --> 00:10:00.190
majority group like white American or white
British. Um, they can be attached to a particular

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:05.670
configuration of nationhood with, uh, ethnic
majority and minority groups, but with a particular,

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:11.610
um, share for each and want to conserve that.
So that's kind of our conservative orientation

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:18.120
based on attachment, which is I think, different
from fearing or hating and outgroup or wanting

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:21.430
to impress an outgroup but wanting to keep
power.

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:26.530
Um, so I think that's sort of how I would,
would state a lot of the impetus. Now, of

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:30.149
course, there are racists in, you know, most
of the populous right voters that clearly

00:10:30.149 --> 00:10:36.100
are going to be people who simply, I hate
Muslims or, or hey, uh, Hispanics or whatever.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:41.290
So, so absolutely. But there's also, we know
from certainly from Ashley Georgina's worked,

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:47.100
for example, on white identity politics. A
lot of voters, for example, a white Americans

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:53.730
who feel warmer towards whites do not feel
cooler towards blacks, let's say, or Hispanics,

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:59.050
then whites who don't particularly feel warm
towards whites. So the warmth towards one's

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:03.390
own group doesn't correlate with hostility
to the outgroup. And I think a lot of this,

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:08.140
there are a lot of people who are attached
to their own group are attached to their own

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:13.140
way of life. And it's not that different actually
from African Americans in Harlem attached

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:17.410
to Harlem's, African American majority. And
so the question is what we do about that,

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:18.839
I think, well before we leave

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:21.721
to what to do about it. I mean, let, let me
make sure I understand that because this is

00:11:21.721 --> 00:11:26.370
an interesting point. If I understand you
correctly, you're saying that you can sort

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:32.180
of rank people based on the level of attachment
they have to their groups. So, for example,

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:36.760
we can take white people in the United States
or as they like to call them, European call

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:42.630
themselves in some groups, European Americans,
so to speak, that you might have one cohort

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:48.029
that has significant sort of attachment and
pride connected to being a white American

00:11:48.029 --> 00:11:52.721
and another cohort which has less pride or
attachment to being a white American, but

00:11:52.721 --> 00:11:59.330
that both of those groups would end up having
a sort of statistically similar feeling towards

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:05.160
other groups like black Americans or Hispanic
Americans or whoever that in other words,

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:11.070
the level of attachment to your group is not
necessarily zero sum when compared to your

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:12.800
feeling towards other groups.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:18.649
Right, exactly. Yeah. That's quite different
site from partisan identity where if you're

00:12:18.649 --> 00:12:23.040
a conservative and you're attached to being
conservative, you have a cooler feeling towards

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:26.140
liberal. So that's much more of a a zero sum
type of identity.

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:32.529
I guess where it becomes politically relevant
is, while that may be, correct me if I'm wrong,

00:12:32.529 --> 00:12:39.870
while at the sort of large scale, uh, in the
aggregate that may be my sense is, and maybe

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:46.540
there is data to back this up, that those
who choose to become activists around keeping

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:53.420
the country white are probably skewed on one
side of those results in terms of how they

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:58.900
feel towards those in the outgroup versus
those who say, you know, my choice of activism

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:03.730
is not going to be around keeping the countrywide,
it's going to be around, for example, increasing

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:07.970
access to healthcare. I'm guessing that there
you would see some dividing lines in terms

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:09.980
of feelings towards outgroups. Okay.

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:15.279
Yeah. I mean I think in the sort of radical
segments like the KKK, you definitely would

00:13:15.279 --> 00:13:21.649
have, you know, high white identity and, and
strong hostility to outgroups. Yes. But I

00:13:21.649 --> 00:13:26.470
think that that I would caution against simply
dismissing the conservative element. I mean

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:32.490
it's not so different from wanting to conserve
a historic building or historic landmarks

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:37.080
or a way of life. I mean that it is going
to get expressed somehow. And I think it would

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:42.720
be better to talk about how quick should we
have this rate of cultural change. How can

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:48.560
the majority adapt? One of the things that
I say in my book is if majority's can be more

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:53.170
open to intermarriage interracial marriage
and adopting of mixed race people into their

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:58.440
group. This is a way forward because I think
you have to give the ethnic majority of vision

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:04.100
of itself in the future. If it's just about
your, the battled past, your toxic, you've

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:08.150
got decline. I don't think that's gonna. I
think that's going to lead to more polarization,

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:13.450
so I think talking about melting the group,
maintaining itself through through interracial

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:16.810
marriage, I think is one, one way forward.
I know that's not going to be accepted by

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:17.810
all members.

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:20.770
Yeah. Well, I mean, it sounds like to some
degree you're saying that there needs to be

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:26.210
some level of appeasement at least of the
fear that is there regardless of what it is

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:30.490
that is motivating that fear that it from
a pragmatic standpoint, which I like to try

00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:34.880
to take with a lot of these issues that just
saying, hey, this is the way it's going to

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:40.390
be and that's, it is not a good path forward
from a pragmatic perspective. But that some

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:45.320
degree of appeasement, and I don't use that
word in a negative way, is uh, probably a

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:46.320
positive thing.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:50.931
No, I mean I wouldn't call it a piece when
so much as a kind of reassurance and not,

00:14:50.931 --> 00:14:55.730
not so much about, I'm not convinced that
it's about fear of loss of power so much.

00:14:55.730 --> 00:15:00.589
I mean you, if you look at Europe where these
groups are more dominant in terms of, you

00:15:00.589 --> 00:15:05.330
know, 90% of the population, it's not really
about that as much as I think a loss of this

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:11.350
attachment to the way things were, the country
they knew growing up, it's not the country

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:16.300
they recognize anymore. That kind of comes
to psychological dissonance is more the the

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:22.920
issue. And I think one way of getting around
that is to talk through how, uh, the consciousness,

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:28.530
the myths of ancestry traditions can continue
through, uh, through a mixed race population

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:33.510
as well, which I'm, I'm arguing that these
white majorities will become over time. I've

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:38.790
kind of Beijing to use Mike Lynn's term, I
think stigmatizing these groups and trying

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:43.260
to sort of frame them as the past and it's
something we have to supersede. That's been

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:48.649
a mistake amongst some progressive actors.
So I think I would prefer it to be a more

00:15:48.649 --> 00:15:54.180
conciliatory move that allows each group to,
of have its identity rather than trying to

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:57.010
talk sify one a identity.

00:15:57.010 --> 00:16:02.440
Very, very interesting stuff. We've been speaking
with Professor Eric Kaufmann, professor of

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:07.060
politics at Birkbeck, University of London
in the UK,

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:12.240
also author of the book white ship, populism,
immigration, and the future of white majorities.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:15.819
Erik, really appreciate your time today.

